.TH Y-NOT 1 "2026-09-02" "y-not 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
y-not \- explain why a user can or cannot access a file or directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B y-not
.I USER OPERATION PATH
.br
.B y-not
.RB { \-\-help | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B y-not
traces the access path to
.I PATH
and reports whether
.I USER
can perform
.I OPERATION
on it, and if not, which component of the path blocks access and why.

Instead of piecing together information from
.BR ls (1),
.BR namei (1),
.BR id (1),
and
.BR getfacl (1),
.B y-not
evaluates owner/group/other permissions, supplementary groups, POSIX ACLs,
Linux capabilities, symlinks and special files, and reports the result in a
single, human-readable explanation.
.SH OPERATION
.TP
.B read\fR, \fBr
Can the user read the file, or list the directory?
.TP
.B write\fR, \fBw
Can the user write to the file, or create/remove entries in the directory?
.TP
.B execute\fR, \fBx
Can the user execute the file, or traverse into the directory?
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Access is allowed.
.TP
.B 1
Access is denied, or an error occurred (unknown user, invalid path, bad
arguments).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Check whether alice can read a file:
.B y-not alice read /srv/project/report.pdf
.TP
Same, using the short form:
.B y-not alice r /srv/project/report.pdf
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B y-not
explains discretionary access control (Unix permissions, ACLs,
capabilities) precisely. SELinux and AppArmor status are reported for
context, but neither changes the allow/deny verdict: SELinux policy
evaluation and AppArmor's per-program confinement do not fit into a
single user/operation/path query.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stat (1),
.BR getfacl (1),
.BR namei (1),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR capability.conf (5)
